---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { citations, citationsOfProduct, dataset, type RankingCategory } from "../lib/rankings";
import { href, indexHref } from "../lib/paths";

const products = new Set(dataset.rankings.map((r) => r.shortName));
const citedProducts = [...products].filter((p) => citationsOfProduct(p).length > 0).length;

const groups: { key: RankingCategory; label: string; blurb: string }[] = [
  {
    key: "overall",
    label: "Overall world rankings",
    blurb: "Whole-of-institution tables ranking every discipline together.",
  },
  {
    key: "subject",
    label: "Subject rankings",
    blurb: "Individual disciplines, where a specialist institution may place far higher than its overall standing.",
  },
  {
    key: "sdg",
    label: "Sustainable Development Goal rankings",
    blurb: "The Times Higher Education Impact tables, assessing contribution to each UN Sustainable Development Goal.",
  },
  {
    key: "thematic",
    label: "Thematic rankings",
    blurb: "Cross-cutting tables measuring a single dimension such as sustainability, employability or institutional youth.",
  },
];

const sourceGroups = groups
  .map((g) => ({
    ...g,
    rankings: dataset.rankings.filter((r) => (r.category ?? "overall") === g.key),
  }))
  .filter((g) => g.rankings.length > 0);
---

<Base
  title="Methodology — The World University Index"
  description="How The World University Index selects each institution's headline world ranking."
>
  <article class="prose container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Editorial standards</p>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      The World University Index consolidates the major international ranking
      systems into a single reference. Our editorial principle is
      straightforward: every institution is entitled to be known by its
      strongest result.
    </p>

    <h2>Selecting the headline ranking</h2>
    <p>
      Universities are assessed by many bodies, each with its own methodology,
      weighting and emphasis — and each publishes not only an overall table but
      rankings by individual subject, by Sustainable Development Goal, and by
      theme. An institution may place differently from one system to the next,
      and dramatically higher in a specialist table than in the overall one.
      Rather than impose a further layer of judgement, the Index reports, for
      each institution, its <strong>best verified world placement</strong> — the
      lowest number it can legitimately claim across every ranking we track,
      whatever its scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      We consider this the most charitable and, we would argue, the most
      defensible reading of the available evidence. If a respected ranking has
      placed an institution at a given position, that position is real, and the
      institution is entitled to cite it.
    </p>

    <h2>Editions</h2>
    <p>
      Rankings are republished each year, and an institution's position can rise
      or fall from one edition to the next. Because a placement a university
      earned in one edition remains a placement it genuinely earned, the Index
      treats each edition as an independent source and reports the strongest
      across all of them. A creditable result is not retired merely because a
      later edition superseded it.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>
      The {dataset.rankings.length} ranking tables currently recorded, grouped by
      scope. Where a publisher serves its tables behind access controls, the link
      points to the relevant ranking portal; granular figures and their sources
      are documented in the project repository.
    </p>
    {
      sourceGroups.map((g) => (
        <section class="source-group">
          <h3 class="source-group__heading">
            {g.label} <span class="source-group__count">{g.rankings.length}</span>
          </h3>
          <p class="source-group__blurb">{g.blurb}</p>
          <ul class="sources">
            {g.rankings.map((r) => (
              <li>
                <a href={indexHref(r.id)}>{r.scope ?? r.name}</a>{" "}
                <span class="sources__meta">
                  {r.shortName} {r.edition} &middot;{" "}
                  <a href={r.url} rel="external">
                    {r.publisher}
                  </a>
                </span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))
    }

    <h2>Which institutions are listed</h2>
    <p>
      An institution appears in the Index once a ranking we track has placed it
      in the world top 50 of any table — overall, by subject, by Sustainable
      Development Goal, thematic or national. We do not maintain a roster of the
      world's universities and then look for their results; the listing is a
      consequence of the results themselves. An institution nobody has ranked
      well anywhere is not one we can report a flattering figure for, and a
      flattering figure is what we are here to report.
    </p>
    <p>
      Institutions are held internally against their <a
        href="https://ror.org"
        rel="external">Research Organization Registry</a
      > identifier rather than their name, since publishers name the same
      institution differently and several quite different institutions share a
      name.
    </p>

    <h2>Which indices count</h2>
    <p>
      A ranking table is included once an institution has been recorded citing
      it. This is a deliberately generous test, and an honest one: the Index does
      not adjudicate whether a ranking is rigorous, well-known or even
      methodologically coherent. It asks only whether the table has been quoted
      by somebody with an interest in the result — which, in practice, is what
      any ranking's authority consists of.
    </p>
    <p>
      Obscurity is therefore not a defect. An institution that cites a ranking
      almost nobody has heard of has demonstrated something about the currency of
      that ranking which a household name never could. Each claim is recorded
      verbatim, with a link to the page it appeared on, so the reader may judge
      it directly. Of the {products.size} indices recorded, {citedProducts} are so
      far attested by {citations.length} published claims — many of them from
      institutions the Index does not otherwise track, since a ranking is
      vouched for by whoever quotes it. The full list is on <a
        href={href("/indices")}>the indices page</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Banded results</h2>
    <p>
      Beyond the upper reaches, several rankings report positions in bands (for
      example, 201–250) rather than exact places. Where a result is banded, the
      Index records the lower bound of the band, consistent with our principle
      of reporting each institution's strongest defensible position.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Figures are refreshed as new editions are published. The dataset is open;
      the update process is documented in the project repository. We welcome
      corrections, particularly from the institutions themselves, who are
      reliably attentive to these matters.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 44rem;
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 4vw, 4rem);
  }

  .prose h1 {
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 1.4rem + 3vw, 3rem);
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose .lead {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
  }

  .prose p {
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
  }

  .source-group {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  .source-group__heading {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.2rem;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.35rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .source-group__count {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .source-group__blurb {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .sources {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }

  .sources li {
    margin: 0.35rem 0;
  }

  .sources__meta {
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
